# November 20, 2026: Lunenburg County, Nova Scotia: Old Town Lunenburg, Mahone Bay's three churches on Edgewater Street,
# Chester, Blue Rocks, Indian Point, the LaHave shore and LaHave Islands, Bridgewater and New Ross. Region ns drawn at random
# by tools/choose.py --claim --state qc,nb,ns,pe,nl. Sources opened in this session: the Canadian Register of Historic Places
# statement for each place (historicplaces.ca, read with tools/page.py --all); the museums' own pages (Lunenburg Heritage
# Society, Fisheries Museum of the Atlantic, Ross Farm Museum, LaHave Islands Marine Museum, Fort Point Museum), the Town of
# Bridgewater's page on the Wile Carding Mill, Lighthousefriends.com on the LaHave light, CBC News (2023) and the Lunenburg
# Academy Foundation's 2026 plan on the Academy, Wikipedia (Wile Carding Mill, Ross Farm Museum, Fisheries Museum of the
# Atlantic, Lunenburg Academy) and Wikipedia's list of historic places in Lunenburg County (Register ids, coordinates).
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'ns_spvy','label':'Lunenburg County, Nova Scotia','added':'2026-11-20 01:13'}
THREE='Mahone Bay\'s "Three Churches" stand side by side on Edgewater Street at the head of the harbour: St. James\' Anglican (1885–1887), St. John\'s Lutheran (1869) and Trinity United (begun 1861, moved here 1885). Sailors lined up the three steeples to steer by.'
MBPARK='Street parking on Edgewater Street; a small mapped lot is on Edgewater Street by St. John\'s Lutheran.'
LUNPARK='Street parking in the Old Town; paid public lots on Bluenose Drive and Lincoln Street.'
PLACES=[
# ---- Lunenburg ----
N('old-town-lunenburg','Old Town Lunenburg','Lunenburg','ns','town','standing',44.3779,-64.3096,'area','public','walk','','1753–',
  'Lunenburg was laid out by Charles Morris on his landing on June 8, 1753, for the "Foreign Protestant" settlers, mostly German-speaking, whom the British brought to Nova Scotia. Its gridiron plan of small lots and four public blocks was the second British "model plan" in what is now Canada and is still almost intact. The town grew on the offshore fishery and shipbuilding; the Old Town became a national historic site in 1991 and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.',
  'Walk the steep grid between the harbour and the hill: wooden houses from the 1700s to the 1900s painted in bright colours, many with the "Lunenburg bump", a dormer pushed out over the front door; single-storey Cape Cod houses and 18th-century houses of "coulisse" (post-in-groove) frame under later clapboard or shingle; Second Empire houses of fishing and shipbuilding families. On the waterfront, the big red sheds and lofts stand gable-end to the harbour, with working shipyards including the Smith and Rhuland yard and the Adams and Knickle complex. Church spires make the skyline (several are separate entries here).',
  'The British model town of 1753 that never lost its grid or its wooden buildings.',
  'Public town streets, open any time. '+LUNPARK+' Most buildings are private homes and businesses: look from the street.',
  [HP('Old Town Lunenburg Historic District National Historic Site of Canada',4256),W('Lunenburg, Nova Scotia')],
  brick='no'),
N('st-johns-anglican-lunenburg','St. John\'s Anglican Church','Lunenburg','ns','worship','standing',44.37812,-64.31114,'exact','view','walk','burned','1754–1763',
  'Built in 1754 to 1763 on one of the four blocks the 1753 town plan set aside for public use, St. John\'s grew with the town: it was enlarged in 1840, through the 1870s and again in 1889, ending as a large white Carpenter Gothic church with twin towers and a rose window. A fire in 2001 gutted it, and it was rebuilt in 2002 to 2004 from the surviving ruins. It was named a national historic site in 1994 as a symbol of the Church of England and British rule in 18th-century Nova Scotia.',
  'A big wooden church under a steep roof, shingle-clad, with pinnacles, turrets, false buttresses and pointed windows, set in an open lawn with paths and a memorial to the first outdoor service held on the site. The Register lists what came through the fire: foundations and ground-floor traces of each building phase, parts of the old walls, façade, central tower and interior columns, fragments of wall and ceiling paintings, stencilling, marbled columns and stained glass, and the altar, pulpit, pews and memorial plaques. Visiting hours and services not checked; interior not checked in this session.',
  'Burned in 2001 and rebuilt around what survived of a 1754 church.',
  'Owned by the Anglican parish. On the town lawn at Cumberland and Townsend streets. '+LUNPARK+' Visiting hours not checked.',
  [HP('St. John\'s Anglican Church National Historic Site of Canada',12344),W('St. John\'s Anglican Church (Lunenburg)')],
  brick='no'),
N('knaut-rhuland-house','Knaut-Rhuland House Museum','Lunenburg','ns','landmark','standing',44.37688,-64.30901,'exact','open','walk','','1793–1794',
  'Built in 1793 to 1794 for Benjamin Knaut, merchant and sheriff, son of one of Lunenburg\'s leading 1753 settlers. The privateer Conrad Rhuland bought it in 1813 and redid the front parlour in the local German manner; from 1823 it was the home of John W. Creighton, a provincial politician, and his family until 1906. The Odd Fellows owned it from 1907, and the Lunenburg Heritage Society bought it in 2000. It became a national historic site in 2002.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey wooden house built right on the Pelham Street sidewalk: five-bay front with a central door, sidelights and transom, six-over-six windows, some of hand-blown cylinder glass, and wide bracketed corner boards. Inside, around two massive central chimneys: early-1800s latches and hinges, mouldings, ceiling cornices and fireplace surrounds. The museum shows life in Lunenburg from 1753 to 1910, with craft demonstrations in summer.',
  '',
  'Owned and run by the Lunenburg Heritage Society; admission by donation. Closed for the season when read in November 2026. Street parking. '+LUNPARK,
  [HP('Knaut-Rhuland House',6488),L('Lunenburg Heritage Society: Knaut-Rhuland House Museum','https://www.lunenburgheritagesociety.ca/museum'),W('Knaut–Rhuland House')],
  brick='?',hours='Victoria Day weekend to August 31, daily 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.; September 1 to 29, daily noon to 4 p.m. (2026 season)'),
N('lunenburg-academy','Lunenburg Academy','Lunenburg','ns','civic','standing',44.38013,-64.31373,'exact','view','walk','','1893–1895',
  'After the county academy burned in 1893, the town built this three-storey wooden school on Gallows Hill to designs by the New Brunswick architect H. H. Mott; it opened in November 1895. It taught primary and secondary grades until 1966 and younger pupils until March 2012, when the school board gave it up and the town took it over. It is the "Castle on the Hill", a national historic site since 1983 (Wikipedia; the provincial register lists it from 1984). The library and a music school moved in; in 2023 the town said it wanted to sell it and offered it to the Lunenburg Academy Foundation, which runs it.',
  'A huge wooden Victorian school painted red, black and white, with a mansard roof of cedar shingle, a massive central chimney, towers on both fronts (the west front has two), pedimented windows, bracketed eaves and decorative shinglework; one tower is missing. The Register lists original wainscoting, stairs, banisters, hinges, doorknobs and the old classroom and cloakroom layout inside. Whether and when visitors may go in was not checked: look from Kaulbach Street and the cemetery next to it.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Town of Lunenburg when last reported (CBC, April 2023), which had offered it to the Lunenburg Academy Foundation; who owns it now not checked. A small mapped lot is on Kaulbach Street beside it. Opening hours of the tenants not checked.',
  [HP('Lunenburg Academy',6483),W('Lunenburg Academy'),L('CBC News (2023): Town looks to sell historic Lunenburg Academy','https://www.cbc.ca/lite/story/1.6814506'),L('Lunenburg Academy Foundation: 2026–2031 strategic plan','https://irp.cdn-website.com/31712816/files/uploaded/LAF+SP_ISSUED_05.21.2026.pdf')],
  th=[44.37998,-64.31335],thn='Kaulbach Street lot',brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('zion-lutheran-lunenburg','Zion Lutheran Church','Lunenburg','ns','worship','standing',44.37913,-64.31213,'exact','view','walk','','1890–1891',
  'Home of Canada\'s oldest Lutheran congregation. The German Lutheran settlers of 1753 worshipped in the open air and then in St. John\'s Anglican; their first church on this site was consecrated in 1772 and replaced in 1840. The present Late Gothic Revival church of 1890 is by the Hamburg-born Halifax architect Henry David Busch, built by George W. Boehner and Sons; Artemus Hall was added behind it in 1946.',
  'A long wooden nave running the whole block on Cornwallis Street, with stained-glass windows and dentil courses, and two unlike towers on the Fox Street front: a tall spire over the corner door, holding the Saint Antoine-Marie bell and seen from the harbour approaches, and a short pyramidal tower with round and lancet windows. Interior not checked; service times not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the congregation. At Fox, York and Cornwallis streets. '+LUNPARK,
  [HP('Zion Lutheran Church',6281),W('Zion Evangelical Lutheran Church (Lunenburg)')],
  brick='no'),
N('st-andrews-presbyterian-lunenburg','St. Andrew\'s Presbyterian Church','Lunenburg','ns','worship','standing',44.37831,-64.30970,'exact','view','walk','','1828',
  'Home of the oldest Presbyterian congregation in Canada. The present church was built in 1828 in place of an earlier one on the same site, next to the block the 1753 plan gave to the town hall, court house and Anglican church. In 1879 it was lengthened to 83 feet and widened to 40, and the spire raised to 118 feet; in 1909 the inside was redone in High Victorian Gothic and a chancel added at the east end.',
  'A white clapboard church with black trim, its nave flush with the street, a central tower with paired gablets under a 118-foot spire, a three-light Gothic window with tracery in the tower, and six hooded Gothic windows of stained glass along the nave. Interior not checked; service times not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the congregation. Corner of Townsend and King streets. '+LUNPARK,
  [HP('St. Andrew\'s Presbyterian Church',1639),W('St. Andrew\'s Presbyterian Church (Lunenburg)')],
  brick='no'),
N('walters-blacksmith-shop','Walters Blacksmith Shop','Lunenburg','ns','industry','standing',44.3761,-64.3058,'site','view','walk','','1893–1902',
  'Thomas Walters bought the corner lot in 1893 and put up the shop soon after; the rear part was added in 1902 and the lean-tos in 1924. The Walters family worked it as a forge until 2005, and it did the ironwork ("shipsmithing") for the schooner Bluenose and for Bluenose II, and for the HMS Rose and HMS Bounty replicas.',
  'A shingled one-and-a-half-storey shop painted deep grey with barn-red trim, dropping a storey to Montague Street, with big eight-over-eight windows the smith worked by, wide barn doors and a louvred cupola. The Register lists inside the two forges with brick chimneys, the plank floor, hooks and hardware made there, and a piece of a ship\'s mast that held the trip hammer. Present use not checked: look from the street.',
  'Where the ironwork for the Bluenose was forged.',
  'Private. Corner of Kempt and Montague streets, in the waterfront industrial blocks. '+LUNPARK,
  [HP('Walters Blacksmith Shop',2056)],
  brick='?'),
N('fisheries-museum-atlantic','Fisheries Museum of the Atlantic','Lunenburg','ns','industry','standing',44.37611,-64.31199,'site','open','walk','','1899',
  'A museum of the Atlantic fishery in a group of red waterfront buildings, run by the Lunenburg Marine Museum Society for the Nova Scotia Museum. The oldest building in the complex was put up in 1899 by W. C. Smith and Company as a salt store for the fishery, later a warehouse and cooperage.',
  'Bright red wharf buildings on Bluenose Drive with fishing vessels tied up alongside (the Cape Sable "money boat" is open to walk aboard), daily talks and demonstrations, and the 1899 salt store, now the entrance and gift shop. Indoors and on the wharf, by ticket.',
  '',
  'Nova Scotia Museum site. Admission charged. A public pay lot is in front of the museum. Hours from the museum\'s visit page, read November 2026; Wikipedia gives the usual season as mid-May to mid-October.',
  [L('Fisheries Museum of the Atlantic: Visit','https://fisheriesmuseum.novascotia.ca/visit'),W('Fisheries Museum of the Atlantic')],
  th=[44.37601,-64.31090],thn='Bluenose Drive lot',brick='?',
  hours='Season about mid-May to October; 2026: daily 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m. to October 24, then 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. to October 31'),
# ---- Mahone Bay ----
N('st-james-anglican-mahone-bay','St. James\' Anglican Church','Mahone Bay','ns','worship','standing',44.4505,-64.38022,'exact','view','walk','','1885–1887',
  'The parish\'s first church, of 1835, stood on the hill above the old Mush-a-Mush burying ground. This one, on a waterfront lot bought in 1848, was built in 1885 to 1887 to designs by the church architect William Critchlow Harris, whose brother Edward was curate and then rector until 1931; John E. Inglis of the congregation oversaw the work. '+THREE,
  'A massive High Victorian Gothic church of board and batten painted yellow ochre with brown and red trim, buttressed, with a 30-metre shingled spire banded in colour at the street corner, a five-light traceried east window and Harris\'s hole-drilled bargeboards. The bell was cast in 1879 at the McLeod Foundry in Mahone Bay; tower chimes, installed in 1986, ring the hours. It is a venue for the summer "Music at the Three Churches" concerts. Interior not checked; service times not checked.',
  'One of the three steeples sailors steered by.',
  'Owned by the Anglican parish. '+MBPARK,
  [HP('St. James\' Anglican Church',11975)],
  th=[44.45143,-64.38031],thn='Edgewater Street lot',brick='no'),
N('st-johns-lutheran-mahone-bay','St. John\'s Evangelical Lutheran Church','Mahone Bay','ns','worship','standing',44.45112,-64.38074,'exact','view','walk','','1869',
  'Mahone Bay\'s Lutherans shared the Union and Harmony Church of 1833 with Baptists, Methodists and Presbyterians until they organised in 1864 and built their own church, ready in 1869: the first of the three on the shore. It is said to have had the first decorated Christmas tree in any church in the province. In 1903 it was widened and simplified, its pointed window arches made round. '+THREE,
  'The middle one of the three: a white frame church with little black trim, round-arched windows and the slender original spire; Sunday-school wings behind date from 1965 and 1970. Interior not checked; service times not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the congregation. '+MBPARK,
  [HP('St. John\'s Evangelical Lutheran Church',12058)],
  th=[44.45143,-64.38031],thn='Edgewater Street lot',brick='no'),
N('trinity-united-mahone-bay','Trinity United Church','Mahone Bay','ns','worship','standing',44.45161,-64.38097,'exact','view','walk','','1861–1885',
  'Built by the Presbyterians from 1861 on the hill behind the old burying ground, next to the first Anglican church, and moved down to the shore road in 1885; it was raised in 1894 for a basement hall. At church union in 1925 most Methodists and Presbyterians joined here as Trinity United, and the Methodist church\'s bell hangs in its tower. Its steeple, once the tallest of the three, was damaged in the late 1920s and not replaced. '+THREE,
  'The most northerly of the three: a white church mixing Gothic pointed windows and belfry openings with a Classical pediment and cornices, on a raised foundation sheathed in wood made to look like stone. The Register lists a Casavant organ, the original wooden pews, wainscoting and wood ceiling inside. Interior not checked in person; service times not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the congregation. '+MBPARK,
  [HP('Trinity United Church',12095)],
  th=[44.45143,-64.38031],thn='Edgewater Street lot',brick='no'),
# ---- Chester ----
N('chester-train-station','Chester Train Station','Chester','ns','rail','standing',44.54709,-64.24442,'exact','open','drive','stations','c. 1905',
  'A full-service station of about 1905 on Mackenzie and Mann\'s Halifax and Southwestern Railway along the South Shore. It had a loading deck that wrapped round it and ran on to the freight shed; the water tower and coal shed were gone before 2000, and so are the tracks. The Chester Municipal Heritage Society runs it, with its office and the area\'s visitor information centre inside.',
  'A shingled station with very wide eaves on big curved braces with scrolled ends, a raised second hipped roof with a steep gable, and little gambrel dormers on every side; double freight doors under seven-pane transoms. The plainer freight shed beside it has the same braced eaves. Inside is a visitor centre; hours not checked.',
  '',
  'Run by the Chester Municipal Heritage Society (Register, 2007), with the Chamber of Commerce and visitor information office inside. At 20 Smith Road. Parking at the station. Opening hours not found on a page read in this session.',
  [HP('Chester Train Station',7327)],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('old-methodist-church-chester','Old Methodist Church','Chester','ns','worship','standing',44.5450,-64.2391,'site','view','walk','','c. 1881–1918',
  'Built about 1881 by the six members of Chester\'s only Methodist congregation, it served as a church only until 1918. A board of trustees took it for the local Scout troop in 1930, and it was the Scout hall for 41 years; since 1971 it has been a private house.',
  'A tall wooden Gothic Revival building at the edge of Main Street with an unusual window pattern: each lancet window is topped by an equilateral triangle made of smaller triangular panes, and a big one of three panes crowns the front door. Greek Revival touches: decorated returned eaves and small capitals on the corner boards. A private home: look from the street only.',
  '',
  'Private residence: view from Main Street only. Street parking in the village.',
  [HP('Old Methodist Church',7236)],
  brick='no'),
# ---- New Ross ----
N('ross-farm-museum','Ross Farm Museum and Rosebank Cottage','New Ross','ns','landmark','standing',44.72707,-64.45588,'site','open','drive','','1816–1817',
  'In August 1816 Captain William Ross brought his family and 172 disbanded soldiers of the Nova Scotia Fencibles to settle the forested interior at Sherbrooke, later New Ross. After a first log house he built the frame house Rosebank in 1817, the first house in the settlement, on his 800-acre grant overlooking Lake Lawson. Ross died in 1822; his widow Mary and their children kept the farm, the family lived in the house into the 1900s, and it joined the Nova Scotia Museum in 1970 as a living-history farm.',
  'Rosebank Cottage, little changed since it was built: a Cape Cod-style house with a big central chimney serving several fireplaces, a kitchen hearth and bake oven still used, and paired six-over-six windows round a central door. The farm has seven historic buildings on sixty acres (the cottage, a cooperage, farm workshop, schoolhouse, stave mill, Larder barn and Ross barn), heritage-breed animals and costumed guides; paths are unpaved.',
  '',
  'Nova Scotia Museum site; admission charged. Parking at the museum on Highway 12 (accessible spaces by the Learning Centre). Hours from the museum\'s visit page as read November 2026; summer hours may be longer and were not posted.',
  [HP('Rosebank Cottage',8748),L('Ross Farm Museum: Visit','https://rossfarm.novascotia.ca/visit'),L('Ross Farm Museum: History of Ross Farm','https://rossfarm.novascotia.ca/stories/history-ross-farm'),W('Ross Farm Museum')],
  brick='?',hours='As read November 2026: Saturday and Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('christ-anglican-new-ross','Christ Anglican Church','New Ross','ns','worship','standing',44.7370,-64.4570,'area','view','drive','','1876–1879',
  'Begun in 1876 under the Rev. Joseph Norwood, who arrived in 1874 and was known locally for healing the sick, and consecrated in 1879 just after he left; nearby Norwood Settlement (renamed Fraxville about 1906) took his name. The master builder and cabinetmaker Joseph Skerry built it and made the pulpit.',
  'A large shingled Gothic Revival church on a knoll above Highway 12 and the Forties Road, unusual for a country church in its ornament: pinnacles with cross finials on nearly every gable, wooden buttresses, moulded pointed windows, and a bell perched above the west gable under its own little roof on posts, with ornate bargeboards. A small chapel behind the chancel is used in winter. Interior not checked; service times not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Anglican parish. At 4 Forties Road; road-side parking not checked.',
  [HP('Christ Anglican Church',10576)],
  brick='no'),
# ---- Bridgewater ----
N('wile-carding-mill','Wile Carding Mill Museum','Bridgewater','ns','industry','standing',44.37628,-64.53217,'site','open','drive','','1860–1968',
  'A water-powered carding mill, where raw wool was combed into rolls for spinning, worked by the Wile family from 1860 to 1968 with hired hands, mostly unmarried women. It stands in Sebastopol, a 19th-century industrial quarter of Bridgewater on Shady Brook, a tributary of the LaHave River. The province owns it and it is run as a museum by the DesBrisay Museum; the town registered it in 2013.',
  'A small mill with a seven-horsepower overshot waterwheel on Shady Brook; Wikipedia describes the machinery as still able to run. Interior not checked in this session.',
  '',
  'Province of Nova Scotia; a museum of the Nova Scotia Museum family. At 242 Victoria Road. Parking not checked: the nearest mapped lot fell on the road itself on the aerial. Hours are those the Town of Bridgewater gave for the 2025 reopening; later seasons not checked.',
  [L('Town of Bridgewater: Wile Carding Mill Museum','https://www.bridgewater.ca/facilities/art-culture/wile-carding-mill-museum'),W('Wile Carding Mill')],
  brick='?',hours='Summer 2025 (from July 1): Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
# ---- LaHave shore and islands ----
N('fort-sainte-marie-de-grace','Fort Sainte-Marie-de-Grâce and Fort Point Museum','LaHave','ns','fort','marker',44.2882,-64.35162,'site','open','drive','forts','1632',
  'After France regained Acadia by the Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye in 1632, Isaac de Razilly built a fort on this point where the LaHave River narrows and made it the capital of the colony; about 40 settlers farmed here, with a mill and a chapel. After Razilly died in 1636 most moved to Port-Royal, and the fort burned in the 1650s. The ground it stood on has since eroded into the river. A lighthouse stood on the point from 1875 to 1954; its keeper\'s house of 1940 became Fort Point Museum, opened in 1972 by the Lunenburg County Historical Society.',
  'Nothing of the fort survives above ground: a Historic Sites and Monuments Board cairn marks the site near where it stood, with views over the river mouth. Beside it, the 1940 lightkeeper\'s house holds the community museum; a small tower of 1990 recalls the old light, and a larger lighthouse loosely modelled on the 1875 one was later joined to the house.',
  'The first capital of Acadia, 1632, now under the river.',
  'Province of Nova Scotia land used by the Lunenburg County Historical Society (Fort Point Museum, 100 Fort Point Road). The cairn and grounds are outdoors; museum hours not found on its own site in this session. Parking on Fort Point Road at the museum.',
  [HP('Fort Sainte Marie de Grace National Historic Site of Canada',13572),L('Fort Point Museum: About us','https://fortpointmuseum.com/about-us'),L('Lighthousefriends.com: LaHave Lighthouse','https://www.lighthousefriends.com/light.asp?ID=1598'),W('Fort Sainte-Marie-de-Grâce')],
  th=[44.28852,-64.35152],thn='Fort Point Road parking',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('lahave-islands-marine-museum','LaHave Islands Marine Museum','LaHave Islands','ns','worship','standing',44.23278,-64.37037,'site','open','drive','','1913',
  'A "marine access" church: built in 1913 by the Boehner Brothers of West LaHave for the Methodists of the LaHave Islands, who came to it by boat, so its front faces the channel between Jenkin\'s Island and Bush Island. At its dedication 78 motor boats tied up at once. It became United in 1925 and held its last service in 1975; the LaHave Islands Marine Museum Society took it over in 1978 for a museum of the inshore fishery and the old congregation.',
  'A small Gothic Revival frame church on a knoll, with pointed windows, a steep front gable and a tower with an open belfry, common in Lunenburg County; the Register pictures its ventilation pulley system. Inside: the museum of island life and the inshore fishery.',
  '',
  'Run by the LaHave Islands Marine Museum Society; free, donations welcome; it can be opened by appointment outside the season. At 100 LaHave Islands Road, across the second bridge from Crescent Beach Road. No mapped lot: parking not checked.',
  [HP('LaHave Islands Marine Museum',5748),L('LaHave Islands Marine Museum: Visitor information','https://www.lahaveislandsmarinemuseum.ca/visitor-information'),L('LaHave Islands Marine Museum: History','https://www.lahaveislandsmarinemuseum.ca/the-museum/')],
  brick='no',hours='June 1 to September 1, daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('st-john-evangelist-lahave-islands','St. John Evangelist Anglican Church','LaHave Islands','ns','worship','standing',44.22387,-64.35981,'exact','view','drive','','1903',
  'Built in 1903 and consecrated in 1908 by the local carpenter Thomas A. Hemeon, credited with five other Lunenburg County churches. Like its Methodist neighbour it was a church for a congregation of fishing families who came by water, so its door faces the sea; the road was built only in 1978, a year after regular services stopped in 1977. The Register (2006) calls it part of the museum complex of the LaHave Islands Marine Museum Society.',
  'A modest Gothic Revival and Queen Anne church on a knoll on Bell Island: clapboard on its original stone foundation, cross-gabled steep roofs, a traceried Gothic window over the door, arched stained-glass windows and a finial on the front gable. The Register lists the remaining fittings of its church days inside. Whether it opens now was not checked.',
  '',
  'At 388 LaHave Islands Road on Bell Island. Ask at the LaHave Islands Marine Museum about going in (not checked). Road-side parking not checked.',
  [HP('St. John Evangelist Anglican Church',5683)],
  brick='no'),
# ---- Blue Rocks and Indian Point ----
N('st-barnabas-blue-rocks','St. Barnabas\' Anglican Church','Blue Rocks','ns','worship','standing',44.35621,-64.24252,'exact','view','drive','','1885–1886',
  'Finished in January 1886 in the fishing village of Blue Rocks, the only Anglican church ever to serve this part of the county. Rather than build anew when the congregation grew, Boehner and Sons of West LaHave added wings to the nave and chancel in 1907. It is still in use.',
  'A small temple-fronted Greek Revival church with its belfry over the gable-end door, dressed with Gothic lancet windows, arched double doors and trefoil ornament. Interior not checked; service times not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Anglican parish. At 1396 Blue Rocks Road; road-side parking not checked.',
  [HP('Saint Barnabas\' Church',5729)],
  brick='no'),
N('union-church-indian-point','Union Church of Indian Point','Indian Point','ns','worship','standing',44.4604,-64.3123,'site','view','drive','','1903–1904',
  'Built in 1903 to 1904 by the people of Indian Point under the foreman Zechariah Hyson and opened in 1904. It has always been shared by more than one congregation, unlike most of the county\'s union churches.',
  'A small Gothic church on a knoll above the road, looking over Mahone Bay: shingled, with its bell tower set to one side of the front and a Celtic cross on the steeple, on a granite foundation. The Register describes the inside framed like the hull of a wooden ship, with iron tie bars meeting at a carved wooden joint, and a double lancet window of Jesus speaking to fishermen. Interior not checked in person; service times not checked.',
  '',
  'At 181 Indian Point Road; road-side parking not checked. Who manages it not checked.',
  [HP('Union Church of Indian Point',5732)],
  brick='no'),
]
